      "name": "clinical_acuity_signal",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.75,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "An acuity score is a genuine analytical lever for triage; it is not merely redundant with the existing urgency field since it offers finer graduation. However values are somewhat compressed (heavy on '2') and the mapping to source is inferential; the trauma/urgent knee injury and viral fever both scored '2' where differentiation would be expected, so groundedness is moderate but defensible."
    },
    {
      "name": "intervention_intensity",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.8,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Captures resource/effort intensity, a distinct and useful dimension for scheduling slot allocation. Values track the text reasonably (simple exam=1, MRI ordered=2). Some overlap in signal with acuity, but it is a warranted separate inference from procedures/tests mentioned."
    },
    {
      "name": "comorbidity_or_complexity_flag",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Complexity flag is analytically valuable for identifying visits needing longer slots or continuity. Grounding is weaker: the annual-exam row labeled 'single_chronic_condition' is unsupported (no chronic diagnosis stated, only mild fatigue), and the post-MI follow-up labeled 'acute_on_chronic_exacerbation' contradicts the text which explicitly says no angina and stable/tolerating well. These are unwarranted assertions."
    },
    {
      "name": "scheduling_action_recommendation",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Directly answers the query's scheduling focus by mapping each visit to a concrete slot/action; highly actionable grouping axis. Recommendations are well-justified readings of the source (emergency for high acuity, longitudinal slot for chronic follow-up, same-day for acute URI). Reasonable and consistent with the text."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.86,
  "source_groundedness": 0.77,
  "rationale": "The added columns collectively provide several genuinely distinct and actionable axes (problem category, acuity, intervention intensity, complexity, and scheduling action) that directly serve a triage-and-scheduling analysis rather than restating the originals. Usefulness is high across the board. Groundedness is generally sound as categorizations and warranted inferences, but the comorbidity_or_complexity_flag exhibits real hallucination: it invents a chronic condition for a clean annual exam and asserts an 'acute_on_chronic_exacerbation' for a patient the text describes as stable, contradicting the source. This drags overall groundedness to good-but-imperfect."
